"Let $P$ be a finite poset. Write $La(n,P)$ for the largest size of a family of subsets of $[n]$ containing no copy of $P$, and let $e(P)$ be the maximal integer $m$ such that every family consisting of all subsets of $[n]$ of sizes $k,k+1,\\dots,k+m-1$ is $P$-free for all $n$ and $k$.\n\n**Bukh's conjecture.** For all finite posets $P$,\n\n$$\nLa(n,P)=e(P)\\binom{n}{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}\\left(1+O(1/n)\\right).\n$$\n\nThe conjecture predicts the asymptotic size of the largest $P$-free family in terms of the largest consecutive block of levels that is always $P$-free. The surrounding discussion indicates that the asserted asymptotic equality is known for infinitely many posets, while it remains open in general."